---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import Icon from '../components/Icon.astro';
---
<BaseLayout title="Probability Calculator for Numbers and Sequences" description="Explore worked probabilities for a uniform 0-9 model: exact numbers, Big and Small, overlapping colors, and independent ordered sequences.">
  <div class="container"><Breadcrumbs items={[{ label: 'Calculator' }]} /></div>
  <header class="page-header container"><p class="eyebrow">The probability desk</p><h1>Count the possibilities.<br /><em>Understand the chance.</em></h1><p>Probability starts with a precise question. Work from the possible outcomes, then see how the calculation changes across an ordered sequence.</p></header>
  <div class="container tool-layout"><section class="tool-panel calculator-panel" aria-labelledby="worked-title"><div class="figure-topline"><span class="eyebrow">A worked example</span><span class="pill">Static preview</span></div><h2 id="worked-title">What is the chance of drawing a 7?</h2><div class="formula-display"><span>1 favorable digit</span><span class="formula-rule"></span><span>10 possible digits</span><strong>= 10<span>%</span></strong></div><p class="calculation-caption">Assuming each digit from 0 to 9 is equally likely.</p><div class="coming-soon"><strong>Interactive calculator coming soon</strong><p>This worked example is fixed. Event selection, ordered sequences, and repeated-trial comparisons will be added next.</p></div></section><aside class="tool-sidebar"><span class="eyebrow">Change the question</span><h2>Change the calculation.</h2><dl class="probability-list"><div><dt>One exact digit</dt><dd>1 / 10 = <strong>10%</strong></dd></div><div><dt>Big or Small individually</dt><dd>5 / 10 = <strong>50%</strong></dd></div><div><dt>Violet (0 or 5)</dt><dd>2 / 10 = <strong>20%</strong></dd></div><div><dt>7, then 7</dt><dd>1 / 10 × 1 / 10 = <strong>1%</strong></dd></div></dl><a class="text-link" href="/number-probability/">Read the full explanation <Icon /></a></aside></div>
  <section class="section container tool-explanation"><div class="prose"><h2>What the model assumes</h2><p>There are ten outcomes, numbered 0 through 9. Each draw is independent and each digit has the same probability. These assumptions describe our educational model; they do not establish the probabilities or fairness of an external service.</p><h2>One draw or several draws?</h2><p>For one draw, count the distinct digits that satisfy the question. For an exact sequence across independent draws, multiply the probability for each position. Big, Big, Small has probability 1/2 × 1/2 × 1/2 = 1/8, or 12.5%.</p><p>That is the probability of the specified order in the next three draws. It is not the probability of finding that sequence somewhere in a longer history. Learn more in <a href="/patterns-and-randomness/">the streaks and randomness guide</a>.</p><h2>Why color groups need care</h2><p>Red and Violet overlap at 0. “Red or Violet” includes six different digits: 0, 2, 4, 5, 6, and 8. Its probability is 60%, rather than the 70% obtained by adding both groups without correcting for overlap.</p><p>See the <a href="/wingo-rules/#color-classifications">complete color classification table</a> and the <a href="https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-3-two-basic-rules-of-probability">OpenStax probability rules</a> for the underlying concepts.</p><div class="takeaway"><p>A calculated probability describes the stated model. It is not a prediction that an event must occur.</p></div></div></section>
</BaseLayout>
